      "latency_ms": 20812.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1076,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance. Its chemical formula is **H₂O**, which means it is made up of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what that actually means and why water is so unique:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\n*   **The Atoms:** Water is formed when two hydrogen atoms bond with one oxygen atom.\n*   **Polarity:** The oxygen atom is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than the hydrogen atoms. This gives water a **polar** molecular structure, meaning one end of the molecule has a slight positive charge and the other has a slight negative charge. This polarity is the reason water is such a great solvent (it can dissolve so many things) and why it sticks to itself (cohesion).\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules move slowly and lock into a rigid, crystalline structure. Interestingly, ice is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state of water we interact with most. It flows and takes the shape of its container.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast that they escape the liquid surface as an invisible gas.\n\n### 3. Key Properties\nWater is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. It also has unique physical properties that make life possible:\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's temperature and keeps the human body from overheating.\n*   **Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on water and helps plants pull water up from their roots against gravity.\n\n### 4. Occurrence\nWater is the most abundant molecule on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet and is found in oceans, lakes, rivers, clouds, and underground aquifers. It is also found in the atmosphere and is a critical component of all known living organisms."
